      "polarity": "prescriptive",
      "cross_ref": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary:avoid-note-omission-imaj7-vim7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "minor-chord-family-minor-pentatonic-preference",
      "narrative": "For min7 chords belonging to the minor chord family (natural minor harmonic field), Benson prescribes deploying lick lines built purely from the minor pentatonic scale without mixture of the major pentatonic: 'our preference is to use the lines over minor chords or chord sequences belonging to the minor chord family.' This preference is not an absolute rule. Chapter 5, p. 210.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p.210"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "minor-chord-minor-pentatonic-over-major-exception",
      "narrative": "Benson explicitly notes that unmixed minor pentatonic lines are not restricted to minor chords: 'This is not a rule. Melodic lines using only the minor pentatonic scale will often work perfectly over major chords such as C6, Cmaj7 and of course C7.' The minor-family preference is a tendency, not a law. Chapter 5, p. 210.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p.210"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "prescriptive",
      "cross_ref": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary:minor-pentatonic-family-preference"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "blues-dominant-minor-blues-scale-application",
      "narrative": "For dominant 7th chords in a blues context, Benson prescribes using the minor blues scale in combination with the major blues scale -- not summarizing all chords into one scale. 'When we mix the major blues scale with the minor blues scale, we cannot summarize all the chords into one scale anymore, we must follow the harmony. In other words, the combination of both blues scales will work for each dominant 7th chord individually.' Chapter 4, p. 151.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:blues-scale-combination"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.151"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "blues-dominant-minor-blues-major-third-caveat",
      "narrative": "When applying the minor blues scale over a dominant 7th chord, Benson warns that the major 3rd must be played quickly within the phrase: 'when mixed with the major blues scale in the same key, we can use it over any dominant 7th chord, bringing a blues vibe to it. (Make sure to play the major 3rd quickly in the phrase, otherwise the melody can sound wrong).' Chapter 4, p. 141.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:blues-scale-combination"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.141"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "blues-dominant-major-blues-scale-scope",
      "narrative": "For dominant 7th chords, Benson prescribes the major blues scale as applicable: 'The major blues scale can be played over any individual major chord, including the major 7th and dominant 7th chords. However, this scale can sound not bluesy, so the trick is to mix it with the minor blues scale in the same key, reaching a bluesy sound and vibe.' Chapter 4, p. 141.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:blues-scale-combination"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.141"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "blues-dominant-single-scale-prohibition",
      "narrative": "Benson proscribes relying on any single blues scale over all dominant 7th chord changes: 'Be careful not to rely on this technique. It will make you sound like a lazy improviser. Your lines will lack variation and will sound stagnant.' The blues scale may summarize all blues chords at once, but doing so is a failure mode. Chapter 4, p. 140.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:blues-scale-combination"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.140"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "proscriptive"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "blues-dominant-minor-blues-not-for-minor-form",
      "narrative": "Benson proscribes applying the same-key minor+major blues combination rule over minor blues form dominants: 'This will not work for minor blues form.' In minor tonality, the C minor blues scale pairs with the Eb major blues scale, not the C major blues scale. Chapter 4, p. 151.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:blues-scale-combination"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.151"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "proscriptive"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "blues-dominant-minor-pentatonic-also-works",
      "narrative": "Benson explicitly notes that unmixed minor pentatonic lick lines also work over dominant 7th chords: 'Melodic lines using only the minor pentatonic scale will often work perfectly over major chords such as C6, Cmaj7 and of course C7.' This applies even without mixture from the major pentatonic. Chapter 5, p. 210.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p.210"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "harmonic-field-avoid-note-tolerated",
      "narrative": "Benson prescribes that dominant 7th chords within the harmonic field (G7/Mixolydian in the key of C) tolerate the natural major scale's perfect 4th avoid note, unlike the Imaj7 and VIm7 chords. 'This scale works perfectly when played over... G7 (Mixolydian), even while using the avoid note. We must be careful with our note choices.' Chapter 7, p. 269.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 7 p.269"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "dorian-harmonic-field-avoid-note-tolerated",
      "narrative": "For Dm7 (Dorian) within the C harmonic field, Benson prescribes that the natural major scale -- including the perfect 4th avoid note -- works without restriction. 'This scale works perfectly when played over Dm7 (Dorian)... even while using the avoid note. We must be careful with our note choices.' The avoid-note proscription applies specifically to the Imaj7 and VIm7 chords. Chapter 7, p. 269.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 7 p.269"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "locrian-harmonic-field-avoid-note-tolerated",
      "narrative": "For Bm7b5 (Locrian) within the C harmonic field, Benson prescribes that the natural major scale including the perfect 4th avoid note works freely. 'This scale works perfectly when played over... Locrian, even while using the avoid note. We must be careful with our note choices.' Chapter 7, p. 269.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 7 p.269"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "lydian-harmonic-field-avoid-note-tolerated",
      "narrative": "For Fmaj7 (Lydian) within the C harmonic field, Benson prescribes that the natural major scale including the perfect 4th works freely: 'This scale works perfectly when played over... Fmaj7 (Lydian), even while using the avoid note.' The avoid-note prohibition is specific to the Imaj7 and VIm7 chords, not all major 7th chord instances. Chapter 7, p. 269.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 7 p.269"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "tonic-major-major-blues-scale-applicable",
      "narrative": "Benson prescribes the major blues scale as playable over any individual major chord including major 7ths: 'The major blues scale can be played over any individual major chord, including the major 7th and dominant 7th chords. However, this scale can sound not bluesy, so the trick is to mix it with the minor blues scale in the same key.' Chapter 4, p. 141.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:blues-scale-combination"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.141"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "tonic-ionian-arpeggio-zone-anchor",
      "narrative": "For Cmaj7, Benson prescribes using the Cmaj7 chord shape as the navigational anchor for Ionian-side fretboard zones in the five-zone system. 'Each zone will be related with a chord shape (Cmaj7 or Am7). The chords will guide us better than just single notes... Make sure to use the root of each chord to guide you in each zone.' These are navigational tools, not harmonic instructions. Chapter 6, p. 246.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:five-zone-fretboard-navigation"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p.246"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "relative-minor-aeolian-zone-anchor",
      "narrative": "For Am7, Benson prescribes using the Am7 chord shape as the navigational anchor for Aeolian-side fretboard zones. 'Each zone will be related with a chord shape (Cmaj7 or Am7).' The Am7 shape serves as a positional memory aid for the relative minor zones, guiding the player horizontally across the fretboard -- 'we visualize vertically but play horizontally most of the time.' Chapter 6, p. 246.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:five-zone-fretboard-navigation"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p.246"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "minor-blues-chord-pentatonic-identity",
      "narrative": "Benson prescribes understanding that the minor pentatonic scale creates a min7 chord (A, C, E, G = Am7), and that C6 and Am7 share the same notes: 'C6 is an inverted Am7 (1st inversion).' These scales must nevertheless be visualized separately for the five-zone fretboard system to function -- 'it is extremely important to visualize them separately.' Chapter 3, p. 108.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:five-zone-fretboard-navigation"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.108"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "minor-blues-substitution-memorize-first",
      "narrative": "For minor 7th chords in a minor blues context, Benson prescribes memorizing substitution tricks before understanding their theory: 'the minor blues form has two different types of chords (minor and dominant), so we must learn some substitution tricks before we start.' And: 'it is not necessary to understand the theory behind it, just memorize them.' The student must treat these as vocabulary items, not derivable rules. Chapter 5, p. 195.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "benson:method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios:superimposition-vocabulary"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-3-technique-arpeggios",
      "references": [
        {
